Title: Take the downlevel psapi names instead of kernel32's K32* copies
Origin: electron-legacy (this repo)

Three of node.exe's fatal static imports are kernel32's K32EnumProcessModules,
K32GetModuleBaseNameW and K32GetProcessMemoryInfo. All three arrived in
Windows 7, so XP, Server 2003 and Vista export none of them, and the loader
refuses the image before the entry point runs.

No source in the tree asks for those names. Four translation units write the
documented ones --

  src/debug_utils.cc                              EnumProcessModules
  deps/uv/src/win/util.c                          GetProcessMemoryInfo
  deps/uv/src/win/process.c                       GetModuleBaseNameW
  deps/v8/src/base/platform/platform-win32.cc     GetProcessMemoryInfo

-- and psapi.h rewrites each one with the preprocessor:

  #ifndef PSAPI_VERSION
  #if (NTDDI_VERSION >= NTDDI_WIN7)
  #define PSAPI_VERSION 2
  #else
  #define PSAPI_VERSION 1
  #endif
  #endif

  #if (PSAPI_VERSION > 1)
  #define EnumProcessModules          K32EnumProcessModules
  #define GetModuleBaseNameW          K32GetModuleBaseNameW
  #define GetProcessMemoryInfo        K32GetProcessMemoryInfo

So this is a configuration question, and PSAPI_VERSION=1 is the SDK's own
answer to it rather than a workaround. The comment above that block reads
"Give teams a choice of using a downlevel version of psapi.h for an OS
versions. Teams can set C_DEFINES=$(C_DEFINES) -DPSAPI_VERSION=1 for downlevel
psapi on windows 7 and higher." It resolves the three from psapi.dll, which has
exported them since NT 4 and which deps/uv/uv.gyp already names on the link
line as -lpsapi, so nothing new is linked.

In target_defaults rather than per target, because the four units are spread
across three of them -- node_lib, libuv and v8_libbase -- and the macro has to
agree everywhere it is read. The entry goes in the *second* 'conditions' key
of target_defaults, at what is line 467 with this series applied. The first,
at line 356, is dead: a GYP file is a Python literal, duplicate keys silently
take the last value, and that one has been unreachable upstream for long
enough that the error_on_warn block inside it has never run.

Unconditional for Windows rather than gated on ia32 and x64, unlike the ETW
and c-ares patches beside it. There is no cost to a current Windows: psapi.dll
there exports these three as forwarders onto kernel32's K32* copies, so the
only difference is one more jump through psapi, and a single code path is
worth more than saving it.

This is also why winwrap carries no shim for the three, which was the first
plan. There would be little for one to stand on: VM_COUNTERS, the structure
NtQueryInformationProcess returns for ProcessVmCounters, appears in no SDK
header, and enumerating modules would mean walking the PEB loader data that
winternl.h explicitly warns against walking -- its advice being to call
EnumProcessModules instead, which is circular when that is the function being
supplied. Reaching for the documented downlevel path costs three lines.

diff --git a/common.gypi b/common.gypi
--- a/common.gypi
+++ b/common.gypi
@@ -465,6 +465,37 @@
     'msvs_cygwin_shell': 0, # prevent actions from trying to use cygwin
 
     'conditions': [
+      # Take the plain psapi names rather than the K32* ones kernel32 grew in
+      # Windows 7. psapi.h redirects EnumProcessModules, GetModuleBaseNameW
+      # and GetProcessMemoryInfo to K32EnumProcessModules and friends whenever
+      # NTDDI_VERSION is Windows 7 or later, which it is here -- so four
+      # translation units that all write the documented name
+      # (src/debug_utils.cc, deps/uv/src/win/{util,process}.c and
+      # deps/v8/src/base/platform/platform-win32.cc) end up importing three
+      # functions from kernel32 that XP, Server 2003 and Vista do not export,
+      # and the loader refuses the image.
+      #
+      # PSAPI_VERSION=1 is the SDK's own switch for this, not a trick: psapi.h
+      # says "Teams can set C_DEFINES=$(C_DEFINES) -DPSAPI_VERSION=1 for
+      # downlevel psapi". It resolves those three from psapi.dll, which has
+      # exported them since NT 4, and which deps/uv/uv.gyp already names on
+      # the link line as -lpsapi.
+      #
+      # So this is a configuration fix rather than a port, and it is why
+      # winwrap carries no shim for the three. There would be nothing for one
+      # to build on: VM_COUNTERS is in no SDK header, and the module list
+      # would mean walking the PEB loader data that winternl.h warns against
+      # walking -- advising EnumProcessModules instead, which is circular when
+      # that is the function being supplied.
+      #
+      # Unconditional for Windows rather than gated on the legacy
+      # architectures, because it costs a current Windows nothing: psapi.dll
+      # there exports these as forwarders onto kernel32's K32* copies, so the
+      # only difference is one more jump, and one code path is worth more than
+      # saving it.
+      [ 'OS=="win"', {
+        'defines': [ 'PSAPI_VERSION=1' ],
+      }],
       # winwrap's MASM forwarders guard their whole body on an architecture
       # define, and both files are in its source list because one generated
       # project holds every configuration at once. Without the define both
